    "native": "<div class=\"slide-native sm-skill sm-check\" style=\"--accent:var(--aws-green);\" data-correct=\"B\" data-multi=\"\" data-rationale=\"ElastiCache for Redis is a fast in-memory data store that provides sub-millisecond latency to power internet-scale applications in real time. The data will not be stored on the instance itself. This choice is ideal for ensuring that the session state information persists across devices. For more information about Well-Architected Framework principles, see Make services stateless where possible within Design Interactions in a Distributed System to Mitigate or Withstand Failures. For more information about using ElastiCache for Redis for session states, see Getting Started tutorial, Building a fast session store for your online applications.\" data-judge=\"{&quot;prompt&quot;: &quot;A developer migrates a web application from an on-premises data center to the AWS Cloud. Authenticated customers use the application from many different clients simultaneously, including laptops, smartphones, and tablets. The application runs on Amazon EC2 instances behind an Application Load Balancer. The instances run in an Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling group. Upon initial testing, users report that they have to reenter information when they use the web application on their laptops and then switch to using their smartphones. Which solution will make the session state information persist across devices?&quot;, &quot;options&quot;: [&quot;The sticky session feature of the Application Load Balancer does not solve the problem across devices. Sticky sessions rely on a cookie that is not going to be consistent across devices. For more information about sticky sessions, see Sticky Sessions for your Application Load Balancer.&quot;, &quot;ElastiCache for Redis is a fast in-memory data store that provides sub-millisecond latency to power internet-scale applications in real time. The data will not be stored on the instance itself. This choice is ideal for ensuring that the session state information persists across devices. For more information about Well-Architected Framework principles, see Make services stateless where possible within Design Interactions in a Distributed System to Mitigate or Withstand Failures. For more information about using ElastiCache for Redis for session states, see Getting Started tutorial, Building a fast session store for your online applications.&quot;, &quot;A local file could store session state information persistently over time, but this does not solve the problem presented. If session state information is stored locally on the instance when a request is sent to another node behind the Application Load Balancer, the other node does not have access to the session state information.&quot;, &quot;Systems Manager State Manager is not for use of session state information management. Systems Manager State Manager is used to manage the state of an instance itself, such as specific instance configurations or software installations. For more information about Systems Manager State Manager, see AWS Systems Manager State Manager user guide.&quot;], &quot;answer&quot;: &quot;ElastiCache for Redis is a fast in-memory data store that provides sub-millisecond latency to power internet-scale applications in real time. The data will not be stored on the instance itself. This choice is ideal for ensuring that the session state information persists across devices. For more information about Well-Architected Framework principles, see Make services stateless where possible within Design Interactions in a Distributed System to Mitigate or Withstand Failures. For more information about using ElastiCache for Redis for session states, see Getting Started tutorial, Building a fast session store for your online applications.&quot;, &quot;why&quot;: &quot;ElastiCache for Redis is a fast in-memory data store that provides sub-millisecond latency to power internet-scale applications in real time. The data will not be stored on the instance itself. This choice is ideal for ensuring that the session state information persists across devices. For more information about Well-Architected Framework principles, see Make services stateless where possible within Design Interactions in a Distributed System to Mitigate or Withstand Failures. For more information about using ElastiCache for Redis for session states, see Getting Started tutorial, Building a fast session store for your online applications.&quot;, &quot;explanations&quot;: {&quot;A&quot;: &quot;The sticky session feature of the Application Load Balancer does not solve the problem across devices. Sticky sessions rely on a cookie that is not going to be consistent across devices. For more information about sticky sessions, see Sticky Sessions for your Application Load Balancer.&quot;, &quot;B&quot;: &quot;ElastiCache for Redis is a fast in-memory data store that provides sub-millisecond latency to power internet-scale applications in real time. The data will not be stored on the instance itself. This choice is ideal for ensuring that the session state information persists across devices. For more information about Well-Architected Framework principles, see Make services stateless where possible within Design Interactions in a Distributed System to Mitigate or Withstand Failures. For more information about using ElastiCache for Redis for session states, see Getting Started tutorial, Building a fast session store for your online applications.&quot;, &quot;C&quot;: &quot;A local file could store session state information persistently over time, but this does not solve the problem presented. If session state information is stored locally on the instance when a request is sent to another node behind the Application Load Balancer, the other node does not have access to the session state information.&quot;, &quot;D&quot;: &quot;Systems Manager State Manager is not for use of session state information management. Systems Manager State Manager is used to manage the state of an instance itself, such as specific instance configurations or software installations. For more information about Systems Manager State Manager, see AWS Systems Manager State Manager user guide.&quot;}}\"><div class=\"kicker\">SESSION 4 &middot; KNOWLEDGE CHECK &middot; QUESTION 2 of 5</div><h2>Check yourself <span class=\"sm-check-hint\">click an option</span></h2><p class=\"sm-check-q\">A developer migrates a web application from an on-premises data center to the AWS Cloud. Authenticated customers use the application from many different clients simultaneously, including laptops, smartphones, and tablets. The application runs on Amazon EC2 instances behind an Application Load Balancer. The instances run in an Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling group. Upon initial testing, users report that they have to reenter information when they use the web application on their laptops and then switch to using their smartphones. Which solution will make the session state information persist across devices?</p><div class=\"sm-opts\"><button class=\"sm-opt\" data-ltr=\"A\" type=\"button\"><span class=\"sm-opt-l\">A</span><span class=\"sm-opt-t\">The sticky session feature of the Application Load Balancer does not solve the problem across devices. Sticky sessions rely on a cookie that is not going to be consistent across devices. For more information about sticky sessions, see Sticky Sessions for your Application Load Balancer.</span><span class=\"sm-opt-m\"></span></button><button class=\"sm-opt\" data-ltr=\"B\" type=\"button\"><span class=\"sm-opt-l\">B</span><span class=\"sm-opt-t\">ElastiCache for Redis is a fast in-memory data store that provides sub-millisecond latency to power internet-scale applications in real time. The data will not be stored on the instance itself. This choice is ideal for ensuring that the session state information persists across devices. For more information about Well-Architected Framework principles, see Make services stateless where possible within Design Interactions in a Distributed System to Mitigate or Withstand Failures. For more information about using ElastiCache for Redis for session states, see Getting Started tutorial, Building a fast session store for your online applications.</span><span class=\"sm-opt-m\"></span></button><button class=\"sm-opt\" data-ltr=\"C\" type=\"button\"><span class=\"sm-opt-l\">C</span><span class=\"sm-opt-t\">A local file could store session state information persistently over time, but this does not solve the problem presented. If session state information is stored locally on the instance when a request is sent to another node behind the Application Load Balancer, the other node does not have access to the session state information.</span><span class=\"sm-opt-m\"></span></button><button class=\"sm-opt\" data-ltr=\"D\" type=\"button\"><span class=\"sm-opt-l\">D</span><span class=\"sm-opt-t\">Systems Manager State Manager is not for use of session state information management. Systems Manager State Manager is used to manage the state of an instance itself, such as specific instance configurations or software installations. For more information about Systems Manager State Manager, see AWS Systems Manager State Manager user guide.</span><span class=\"sm-opt-m\"></span></button></div><div class=\"sm-check-fb\" style=\"display:none\"></div><div class=\"sm-source\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-circle-question\"></i> Knowledge check &middot; Exam Strategy Session 4 Answer Key</div></div>",
