    "native": "<div class=\"slide-native sm-skill sm-check\" style=\"--accent:var(--aws-green);\" data-correct=\"B,E\" data-multi=\"1\" data-rationale=\"A Lambda alias is a pointer to a specific Lambda function version. For more information about creating and using Lambda function aliases, see Lambda function aliases.\" data-judge=\"{&quot;prompt&quot;: &quot;Each time a developer publishes a new version of an AWS Lambda function, all the dependent event source mappings need to be updated with the reference to the new version’s Amazon Resource Name (ARN). These updates are time consuming and error-prone. Which combination of actions should the developer take to avoid performing these updates when publishing a new Lambda version? (Select TWO.)&quot;, &quot;options&quot;: [&quot;A Lambda event source mapping can either point to the version ARN, or an alias ARN, but not to the layer ARN. For more information about managing Lambda function versions by using an ARN, see Lambda function versions. For more information about creating and using Lambda function aliases, see Lambda function aliases. For more information about Lambda layers, see Creating and sharing Lambda layers.&quot;, &quot;A Lambda alias is a pointer to a specific Lambda function version. For more information about creating and using Lambda function aliases, see Lambda function aliases.&quot;, &quot;This solution does not address your problem. Every alias has its own unique ARN. Therefore, you would still need to update the event source mapping with the new ARN when a new version is published. For more information about creating and using Lambda function aliases, see Lambda function aliases.&quot;, &quot;A Lambda alias cannot point to another Lambda alias, only to a Lambda function version. For more information about Lambda function aliases, including their creation and management, see Using aliases.&quot;, &quot;Instead of using ARNs for the Lambda function in event source mappings, you can use an alias ARN. You do not need to update your event source mappings when you promote a new version or roll back to a previous version. For more information about creating and using Lambda function aliases, see Lambda function aliases.&quot;], &quot;answer&quot;: &quot;A Lambda alias is a pointer to a specific Lambda function version. For more information about creating and using Lambda function aliases, see Lambda function aliases.; Instead of using ARNs for the Lambda function in event source mappings, you can use an alias ARN. You do not need to update your event source mappings when you promote a new version or roll back to a previous version. For more information about creating and using Lambda function aliases, see Lambda function aliases.&quot;, &quot;why&quot;: &quot;A Lambda alias is a pointer to a specific Lambda function version. For more information about creating and using Lambda function aliases, see Lambda function aliases.&quot;, &quot;explanations&quot;: {&quot;A&quot;: &quot;A Lambda event source mapping can either point to the version ARN, or an alias ARN, but not to the layer ARN. For more information about managing Lambda function versions by using an ARN, see Lambda function versions. For more information about creating and using Lambda function aliases, see Lambda function aliases. For more information about Lambda layers, see Creating and sharing Lambda layers.&quot;, &quot;B&quot;: &quot;A Lambda alias is a pointer to a specific Lambda function version. For more information about creating and using Lambda function aliases, see Lambda function aliases.&quot;, &quot;C&quot;: &quot;This solution does not address your problem. Every alias has its own unique ARN. Therefore, you would still need to update the event source mapping with the new ARN when a new version is published. For more information about creating and using Lambda function aliases, see Lambda function aliases.&quot;, &quot;D&quot;: &quot;A Lambda alias cannot point to another Lambda alias, only to a Lambda function version. For more information about Lambda function aliases, including their creation and management, see Using aliases.&quot;, &quot;E&quot;: &quot;Instead of using ARNs for the Lambda function in event source mappings, you can use an alias ARN. You do not need to update your event source mappings when you promote a new version or roll back to a previous version. For more information about creating and using Lambda function aliases, see Lambda function aliases.&quot;}}\"><div class=\"kicker\">SESSION 4 &middot; KNOWLEDGE CHECK &middot; QUESTION 5 of 5</div><h2>Check yourself <span class=\"sm-check-hint\">select all that apply</span></h2><p class=\"sm-check-q\">Each time a developer publishes a new version of an AWS Lambda function, all the dependent event source mappings need to be updated with the reference to the new version’s Amazon Resource Name (ARN). These updates are time consuming and error-prone. Which combination of actions should the developer take to avoid performing these updates when publishing a new Lambda version? (Select TWO.)</p><div class=\"sm-opts\"><button class=\"sm-opt\" data-ltr=\"A\" type=\"button\"><span class=\"sm-opt-l\">A</span><span class=\"sm-opt-t\">A Lambda event source mapping can either point to the version ARN, or an alias ARN, but not to the layer ARN. For more information about managing Lambda function versions by using an ARN, see Lambda function versions. For more information about creating and using Lambda function aliases, see Lambda function aliases. For more information about Lambda layers, see Creating and sharing Lambda layers.</span><span class=\"sm-opt-m\"></span></button><button class=\"sm-opt\" data-ltr=\"B\" type=\"button\"><span class=\"sm-opt-l\">B</span><span class=\"sm-opt-t\">A Lambda alias is a pointer to a specific Lambda function version. For more information about creating and using Lambda function aliases, see Lambda function aliases.</span><span class=\"sm-opt-m\"></span></button><button class=\"sm-opt\" data-ltr=\"C\" type=\"button\"><span class=\"sm-opt-l\">C</span><span class=\"sm-opt-t\">This solution does not address your problem. Every alias has its own unique ARN. Therefore, you would still need to update the event source mapping with the new ARN when a new version is published. For more information about creating and using Lambda function aliases, see Lambda function aliases.</span><span class=\"sm-opt-m\"></span></button><button class=\"sm-opt\" data-ltr=\"D\" type=\"button\"><span class=\"sm-opt-l\">D</span><span class=\"sm-opt-t\">A Lambda alias cannot point to another Lambda alias, only to a Lambda function version. For more information about Lambda function aliases, including their creation and management, see Using aliases.</span><span class=\"sm-opt-m\"></span></button><button class=\"sm-opt\" data-ltr=\"E\" type=\"button\"><span class=\"sm-opt-l\">E</span><span class=\"sm-opt-t\">Instead of using ARNs for the Lambda function in event source mappings, you can use an alias ARN. You do not need to update your event source mappings when you promote a new version or roll back to a previous version. For more information about creating and using Lambda function aliases, see Lambda function aliases.</span><span class=\"sm-opt-m\"></span></button></div><div class=\"sm-check-submit\"><button class=\"sm-submit\" type=\"button\" disabled>Submit</button></div><div class=\"sm-check-fb\" style=\"display:none\"></div><div class=\"sm-source\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-circle-question\"></i> Knowledge check &middot; Exam Strategy Session 4 Answer Key</div></div>",
